  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Thursday: A ridge will be forming along the \nContinental Divide. It will press the moisture plume west and \nnorth bringing a drying trend to the region. Residual moisture \nin the boundary layer is expected to bring an increase in fog \nand low ceilings for valleys and portions of the Basin. Highs \nfor the period will be in the 50s to low 60s. Lows will be in \nthe upper 30s and 40s.\n\nFriday through Tuesday: The ridge axis shifts East allowing for\na series of Lows to push through the region. The track of the \nLows are south of the Pacific Northwest. It is leading to drier \noutlook than previous forecasts. Precipitable water values \nstruggle to get above an inch with the systems. Lowland areas\nhave a 25-75 probability of 0.2-0.5 of an inch Friday through\nTuesday. Mountains have a higher spread of 0.5-1 inch. The \nperiod will trend cooler with snow levels dropping to around \n5000 ft by Saturday morning. Cascade Passes can expect snow. \nStevens having a 25-75 percent probability of 1 to 4 inches and \nWashington Pass with a possible 3 to 7 inches from Friday into \nMonday. Basin winds are breezy during Friday and Saturday \nafternoons with gusts into the 20-25 mph range. Highs for the \nperiod will be in the 40s to low 50s. Lows will be in the upper \n20s and 30s. /JDC",
